00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:04.720
For Dune's score, Zimmer wanted to invoke&nbsp;
sounds that felt like they came from a place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:10.240
thousands of years in the future he didn't want&nbsp;
the audience to ever hear a full orchestra and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:14.800
instead wanted to focus on using sounds that&nbsp;
felt like they came from the world of Dune.

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:20.000
Typically in a score, you might&nbsp;
have a melodic theme or a motif&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:24.320
represent a character or a place but&nbsp;
for this adaptation of Dune Zimmer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:28.560
leans away from that. He definitely&nbsp;
uses certain melodic or rhythmic themes:

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:32.480
Like the rhythmic drum hit,

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:36.320
repeated throughout the film,

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:42.800
in a variety of ways. Often&nbsp;
prefacing action or conflict

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:49.200
But Zimmer focuses more on&nbsp;
connecting certain kinds of sounds to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:53.360
groups influences or settings and then using those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:59.600
sounds to invoke those things he relates the&nbsp;
insane grinding rhythmic throb with the Harkonnen.

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:06.080
He uses this whispery female&nbsp;
chanting for the Bene Gesserit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:10.560
House Atreides gets more&nbsp;
traditional, warmer sounds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:16.400
"I told my father I didn't want this either."&nbsp;
So while Duncan Idaho has his own little theme,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:20.800
it also has a sonic connection to the sound&nbsp;
Zimmer is using for House Atreides as a whole.

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:29.840
And the desert and the Fremen get higher&nbsp;
pitch sounds that invoke wind and sand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:33.520
wind instruments like flutes, as&nbsp;
well as vocalization that is distinct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:36.240
from the whispery high-pitched&nbsp;
singing of the Bene Gesserit.

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:46.160
So this is where it gets really brilliant&nbsp;
I think. Dune is a complicated story with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:50.960
lot of different things going on. There are&nbsp;
many competing forces, Paul, as a character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:55.600
is struggling between different identities and&nbsp;
callings. And associating different sounds and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.600 --> 00:04:00.480
instruments with different ideas allows Zimmer&nbsp;
to actually bring these different forces into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:06.240
conflict within a single piece or melody. For&nbsp;
example: once we associate this sound with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:14.160
Bene Gesserit and this kind of sound with House&nbsp;
Atreides and the Duke, listen to how these two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:19.680
ideas come into play in the score during&nbsp;
this conversation alongside the dialogue.&nbsp;

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:24.960
"Will you protect our son?"
"With my life."&nbsp;

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:27.840
"I'm not asking his mother&nbsp;
I'm asking the Bene Gesserit."&nbsp;

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:33.760
When the Duke addresses Jessica as the Bene&nbsp;
Gesserit we hear the Bene Gesserit sounds come in.

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:39.120
"Will you protect Paul?"
And then when she tries to reassure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:46.400
him we hear the House Atreides sound come in –but&nbsp;
it comes in on top of the Bene Gesserit sound. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:51.120
Zimmer had been relying here on a melody to&nbsp;
communicate these two ideas this would have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:56.720
been a lot more difficult, but instead, both&nbsp;
can exist within the same piece simultaneously.

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:05.040
Another example is this scene: the higher-pitched&nbsp;
dentist drill sound represents the pain being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:10.160
inflicted on Paul's hand by the box, and the&nbsp;
vocalizations represent something being awakened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:15.760
deep within Paul. These two forces can actually&nbsp;
exist and come into conflict within the piece.

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:21.920
So we have the desert, the Bene Gesserit, the&nbsp;
Fremen, House Atreides, and various prophecies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:26.640
represented in the score and Paul struggles&nbsp;
internally between all these roles and identities.

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:34.880
In the book, we can actually read this&nbsp;
play out in his internal monologue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:39.520
but in the film, they decided to stay away&nbsp;
from any kind of voice-over and so the score&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:44.560
helps bring that kind of internality onto the&nbsp;
screen.And that's not to say the score isn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:49.760
utilizing melodic themes, but Zimmer instead&nbsp;
of linking melodies to specific characters

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:52.320
(with the exception of maybe Duncan Idaho)

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:55.440
uses them more to illustrate plot lines.

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:59.760
There are complementary melodic motifs&nbsp;
that follow Paul throughout his journey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:04.960
into the desert and because Zimmer is using&nbsp;
sound for locations and groups instead of melody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:10.320
he can evolve how he's presenting that melody&nbsp;
depending on where Paul is in his journey. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:16.000
example: we're first introduced to this theme&nbsp;
that follows Paul's journey into the desert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:20.240
with the warmer tones used for house&nbsp;
of tradies but there's also a sense of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:26.400
mournfulness and longing as he awakens from&nbsp;
dreams of the planet arrakis and the desert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:31.200
and then when paul is learning about the&nbsp;
fremen and the desert on arrakis we get this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:35.600
same theme but it's a bit distorted as if&nbsp;
we're only getting a vague picture of the place

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:46.400
finally when paul is flying over the desert we&nbsp;
get the theme again but this time it's no longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:53.760
distorted it has a new intensity and it's lacking&nbsp;
that long quality as if to say this is my new home&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:57.200
this melody marks a certain element&nbsp;
the plot and the way it's portrayed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.200 --> 00:07:02.160
sonically gives that plot an emotional&nbsp;
arc zimmer uses another complimentary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:06.480
melodic motif to represent pieces&nbsp;
of this same journey into the desert

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:11.920
look at how this moment

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:19.200
is linked to this one with both&nbsp;
a visual and a melodic motif

00:07:28.480 --> 00:07:33.040
while the soundscape of voices and&nbsp;
instruments that zimmer uses for each moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.040 --> 00:07:38.720
represents the two environments distinctly&nbsp;
these two complementary themes come together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:44.720
in the final scene as paul advances even&nbsp;
deeper into the desert the track for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:51.120
leaving calidan and the film's climax are very&nbsp;
similar but while paul is in kaladan the vocals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:57.120
play more of a background role while the track&nbsp;
leans more heavily on the aggressive bagpipe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.120 --> 00:08:02.400
style sounds that were associating with house&nbsp;
atreides but for the film's climax in the desert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:11.840
those sounds are still there but they fall to&nbsp;
the background while the vocals take the lead

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:17.200
another example of how this plays out is&nbsp;
with the benny desert order while they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:21.040
don't spend much time on screen they play&nbsp;
a huge role in the story behind the scenes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:24.480
they're not visually present in&nbsp;
the destruction of arakin but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:30.720
we know from the story they've played a part in&nbsp;
